{
  "rules": [
    "Model exhibits severe underestimation during high-volatility regimes; when true outcomes spike >5% above the last known price (e.g., +4-6 EUR), forecasts remain flat or drift slowly upward, failing to capture immediate momentum.",
    "Forecasts show a strong mean-reversion bias toward the historical average (~70.8) even after significant positive shocks, whereas true outcomes often sustain elevated levels for 3-5 days before correcting.",
    "Sentiment spikes (1.0) preceding price surges are ignored by the model; forecasts do not adjust upward in magnitude when sentiment history indicates bullish consensus despite flat historical prices.",
    "Model fails to account for 'mean reversion from highs' correctly; after a sharp spike, true outcomes often drop below the forecasted path within 10 days, but the model continues its slow linear ascent.",
    "Forecast horizon is too smooth and lacks intra-period volatility; true outcomes display erratic swings (e.g., +6 EUR in one day, -4 EUR next), while forecasts predict monotonic or near-monotonic trends."
  ]
}
